speechtext:
He dares to justify it. I made the charge the other night that the minister, having directed Mr. Bain to do this work, and having knowledge that this work was being done by a man in his department, whom he had promoted over the heads of other men of longer standing in the department and at least as worthy of promotion, and that this man was taking his salary of $2,800 from the public revenue and employing his time writing campaign literature for the Liberal party, but yet the hor. gentleman says there was nothing discreditable about that. His party have had com mission after commission going through the length and breadth of this country trying to tind cause to dismiss Conservatives who were holding office when hon. gentlemen opposite came into power. And why did they dismiss them ? Because they had voted as their consciences had dictated, although they were men who had never taken any part in politics. Expensive commissions were appointed to try these men, who were put to tremendous expense to defend themselves. In one case in my own county, officials had to spend $500 or $600 to, defend themselves from a charge of partisanship. They had done nothing but vote as their consciences dictated. And here we have a man, a minister of the same party, attempting to justify and saying there is nothing discreditable in the conduct of a man in receipt of $2,800 of the public revenue of this country in doing what I have charged. The hon. gentleman admitted it, because he could not deny it and dare not deny it, because he knew that the man was sitting within the sound of his own voice, who knew that be knew that that work is being done and yet he says there is nothing discreditable or wrong in it. I was surprised although I thought the time had passed when one could be surprised at any statement that might come from hon. gentlemen opposite, but there is still room for surprise-when I heard the hon. gentleman (Mr. Paterson), from whom we might naturally expect better things, make a statement of this kind. Coming from some of the colleagues of the hon. gentleman it would not cause as much surprise as it did coming from him, because from him we expected better things. Yet he says there is nothing wrong about it,

and want to know wliat I mean by speaking of this partisan as a political hack. I meant exactly what I said, and what I say now;
1	thought I put it as plainly as it could be put in the English at my command. It is wrong, it is discreditable. Judged by the standards the hon. gentlemen laid down, it is very discreditable on the part of any civil servant, employed and paid out of the public treasury, to devote his time to writing political campaign literature for any political party, whether Liberal or Conservative.
